  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=(-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-3)\\cdot (3)=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular.",
